This sounds like you solved the game balance problem so well refilled
potions are as useful as scrolls of mail. Which means not at all.
Probably the biggest merits of your patch as-is will be it relieves
Nethack (variant) maintainers of yet another recurring idea, and to
provide flavor to a game that lives off things like this.

On the upside, I am very glad to see people doing Nethack development.

If you aren't turned off by my caustic review, may I politely suggest
refilled potions do something interesting? Perhaps they leak
occasionally or last only a finite time. They could act like ordinary
potions, but much less liquid stored inside. Perhaps wimpy monsters
get a potion more frequently, but then already-refilled. Or drinking
one can unleash a water demon / potion golem. Some have sailships
inside, with which you could really sail for 2 turns. Maybe
shopkeepers will throw you out for importing cheap vodka (tax evasion)
and curse in Swedish. Orcs and demons will play with mankind's toys,
and can be lured away with strategically placed glittering bottles.
Empty, non-refilled bottles go to trash collection if you don't refill
them fast enough.

Counteracting "too many available potions"  could also mean different
and rather weak refilled effects instead of no net gain in potion
number. E.g. effects similar to potions' vapors. Or object detection
could detect objections to currently-held conducts. Monster detection
could detect the monster inside you (berserk technique). Gain energy
jolts you with electricity and any foes you touch for 1d2 damage.
Fruit juice has you pay 1/3 your money to Coca Cola Inc. in return for
bad breath. Enlightenment slightly increases chances of reading a
spellbook. Booze, offered to a succubus, makes the encounter slightly
more enjoyable, but drunk yourself makes you play the Nethack Quaffing
Game with lots of stupid challenges. Poison makes the next piece of
food you eat significantly older. Healing provides you with a healing
attack similar to a nurse. Scrolls dipped in confusion turn to confuse
monster. Gain level makes you cast spells 1 skill level up instead for
the next 10 turns, i. e. selecting dig in the menu would result in
polymorph etc. Acid makes you hallucinate shortly - all monsters are
displayed as random L, S, D (but yellow molds cure this). Restore
ability undoes 1/3 the hit point damage (and regeneration) both you
and all monsters took in the past turn. Para-Lysis makes you split
(lys) an oppo-(para)-nent off yourself, such as a hostile quest
guardian. Gain ability enables you to gain (weight? something related
to Star Trek? ability to lay one egg?). Levitation gets you a
temporary alignment adjustment, since Levi were ancient Israel's
priest class. Sleeping puts your character on autopilot for some 50
turns, i. e. make a TAEB bot take over control.

Perhaps refilled potions function entirely on color, not
"description". You could stain the floor with a purple potion for a
semi-permanent Elbereth. A swirly potion makes 1 square of stench
(stinking cloud) for 5 turns.

Hmm. I hope you get the idea. At least it sure was fun thinking up
this rant.


